WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
Film Courage: Have you always had&nbsp;
thick skin when it comes to writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.840
criticism? Travis Seppala, Author/Screenwriter:&nbsp;
No, I'm not even convinced I have thick skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.880
now because I'm very good at taking a&nbsp;
note from someone and trying to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.920
like how to fix it or if I don't understand the&nbsp;
note like how to find the note within the note&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:25.920
sort of thing and see what the deeper problem is&nbsp;
to fix it overall. But there are still some notes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.120
that make me upset where I'm like how could you&nbsp;
not understand this? It's right there on the page.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:40.000
Yeah so it doesn't happen as frequently as it&nbsp;
did when i was younger and was newer but I try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.600
to keep that at bay or if something even irks me&nbsp;
just a little bit like thank you for the note and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.760
then I'll come back to it later and be like okay&nbsp;
maybe there was something there? Let's take a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:56.960
at it and calm myself down. But nobody's perfect&nbsp;
and I sure as heck I'm not either. Film Courage:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.240
What would you tell someone who wanted to quit&nbsp;
their day job today so that they could pursue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.960
screenwriting full-time? Travis: Don't do&nbsp;
that, don't do that. Again like there's I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:15.200
couldn't find the chapters in the book in a quick&nbsp;
but you're not going to get rich right away and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.680
you're definitely not going to get rich right&nbsp;
away without having some way to pay your rent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.840
and there's no reason to go homeless in the&nbsp;
pursuit of it. There are plenty of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.240
what's her name? JK Rowling [Harry Potter&nbsp;
author] she was going again how many hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.720
rejections before her book finally got picked up?&nbsp;
If she didn't have her job like she would've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.080
homeless. Even more because i've read stories&nbsp;
where she couldn't feed her kids or whatever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.080
It's like that would have been even worse&nbsp;
if she had quit her job to write that book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.480
You don't quit your job until you even...and even&nbsp;
once you've had a success unless it's a TV thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:57.040
where now your new job is writing in a writer's&nbsp;
room because like I said even if you sold your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.640
script for a hundred thousand dollars after you&nbsp;
pay all your stuff you got like forty thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.680
dollars and how much are you making in the year&nbsp;
right? Compare that to your job. Sure if you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.840
to take a year off but don't if you're quitting it&nbsp;
to start pitching stuff that's insane. I'm sorry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:18.800
you need to pay rent. You need to eat. You need&nbsp;
to feed your kids and family, if you have that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:27.360
Your dog would appreciate having some food as&nbsp;
well. Do not quit your job until you are making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.560
enough of an impact where you can just survive on&nbsp;
the screenwriting. Film Courage: What do you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:40.080
20-year-olds to know about screenwriting? Travis:&nbsp;
20-year-olds all right. It's been a long time for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:49.840
me I'm 36 now so not everything has to be&nbsp;
a video game or comic book in movie form.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.960
Those are great but not every movie has to be and&nbsp;
they're not going to buy your superhero script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.000
unless it's from an IP [intellectual property].&nbsp;
I've made the mistake of writing my own superhero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.400
and nobody cares because it's not, it doesn't&nbsp;
already have the audience you know. That's whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.640
horror you can come up with original horror movies&nbsp;
and it's awesome. We want to see it. You can come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.040
up with awesome action movies. It's original.&nbsp;
We want to see it. Don't write adaptations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.440
Don't write adaptations of things you don't&nbsp;
own the rights to. Don't write an original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:25.760
superhero movie because nobody cares unless it's&nbsp;
an adaptation. It's a very interesting thing. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.720
if you can get the rights to a superhero by all&nbsp;
means get the rights and then write it but don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.280
write your own superhero and don't try to writing&nbsp;
that adaptation of something you don't own the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:41.840
rights to. I've seen a lot of people in the forums&nbsp;
like on Facebook who are wanting to write their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.720
own Spider-Man movie. You don't own the rights&nbsp;
of Spider-Man. No one will ever see that in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.480
they may even try to sue you if you try sending&nbsp;
it to the people who do own the rights to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.440
I made that mistake once again when I was first&nbsp;
starting out I wrote a superhero a superman script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.800
not knowing I couldn't do anything with it. I&nbsp;
wrote an adaptation of the jayjay monologues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.000
not knowing that I couldn't do that and I got in&nbsp;
touch with people, found out the rights already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.800
owned, so blah blah blah it didn't work. That's&nbsp;
the thing don't adapt something you don't own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:18.000
because you're just gonna be disappointed and&nbsp;
waste your time. Film Courage: What about advice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.640
to 20-year-olds on mindset? Such as Don't get&nbsp;
too high or too low. I think when you're younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:29.120
and you come here and everybody's so in awe of&nbsp;
young people in Hollywood. It's easy to really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.120
to go on these roller coaster rides because people&nbsp;
are telling you how amazing you are or they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.000
either telling you you're not and then they just&nbsp;
told you yesterday you were. Travis: Right So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:48.800
don't let rejection hurt you too bad because&nbsp;
they're not necessarily rejecting you the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:55.520
person they're rejecting your script and a lot of&nbsp;
people seem to take a rejection of the script as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:01.840
rejection of them personally. If because I didn't&nbsp;
like your script I hate you? I don't hate you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:08.240
I don't know you. You could be a wonderful person&nbsp;
but I don't like your script. It's not a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:14.960
story. I'm sorry but it has nothing to do with the&nbsp;
person. Don't let the rejections be personal for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:22.800
you and on the flip side don't rejoice at every&nbsp;
minor success or something that even looks like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.280
minor success because it can still fall through.&nbsp;
I can't tell you how many times I've had this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:33.200
amazing looking deal that then just gets yanked&nbsp;
out from under me for whatever reason. I had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:39.280
producer a few years ago who was going to pay&nbsp;
me like $20,000 to write a pirate movie for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.960
and then all of a sudden his investor who was&nbsp;
giving him it was for like an $80,000,000 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:50.400
dollar budget or something like that and then he&nbsp;
was like Oh I have the money blah blah. I'm gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.120
send you the stuff I'm going to send you the&nbsp;
$20,000 check to start writing like next week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.760
Didn't hear from him. Next week didn't get a check&nbsp;
so I call him up oh yeah so my investor dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:10.480
out or another one where I was supposed to get&nbsp;
hired to write a sequel to a horror movie and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:15.840
the producer just stopped communicating with me&nbsp;
all of a sudden, no reason given, just stopped.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:22.960
I even had one producer who literally died&nbsp;
during our negotiations so that didn't happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:27.520
you know. So it's like there's always always&nbsp;
something that can make it go wrong. So don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:33.840
start celebrating until the check is cleared&nbsp;
is the rule of thumb i've got on that. Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:40.160
it's fabulous that people are interested in my&nbsp;
stuff but until like there's a contract signed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.800
money in my hand it could go wrong at any time and&nbsp;
even after then they're still playing movies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.760
sell for millions of dollars the script and&nbsp;
then the movie never comes out you know. so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:57.040
Stuff dies in development hell every day&nbsp;
you know. Nothing is a success until it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:03.040
100 successful. Film Courage: What's your&nbsp;
favorite movie about the industry? Travis:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:08.160
I really like Trumbo. That was a really&nbsp;
fantastic movie. Film Courage: Why did you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.120
like it? Travis: Aside from Brian Cranston's&nbsp;
amazing in almost everything he's in. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:20.480
that new Mountain Dew commercial. Are you kidding&nbsp;
me? He's fantastic. Seeing how the industry was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:24.720
at that time is interesting to learn there's&nbsp;
been other movies that have shown that like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.160
you may have not seen the Majestic with&nbsp;
Jim Carrey where he played a screenwriter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.960
who ended up getting blacklisted because they&nbsp;
thought that he was a communist or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:43.040
and there's the same time period and so it's&nbsp;
very interesting to see how all the world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:52.560
Hollywood is being shaped by hearsay and then to&nbsp;
still see how Trumbo and his friends kind of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:58.400
circumnavigated the issue in a creative clever&nbsp;
way. They took a huge cut in their paychecks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:05.680
but they still did what they love you know&nbsp;
and it was just a really interesting look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:11.840
what the the industry was like back then&nbsp;
and then how people could get around that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:25.840
and how they all changed today and then&nbsp;
again Brian Cranston's a freaking genius.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:35.280
Question For The Viewers: How do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:36.738
you handle criticism to your writing?

